13.3  Redirections and file descriptors

TL;DR
  - Every program starts with three open files: 0 is stdin (its input), 1 is
    stdout (its output), 2 is stderr (its errors).
  - > sends stdout to a file, replacing it; >> appends; < reads stdin from a
    file.
  - 2> redirects errors, 2>&1 sends them where stdout goes, and | connects
    stdout to the next program's stdin.

      make > build.log 2>&1              # everything into one file
      find / -name '*.conf' 2>/dev/null  # hide the errors
      ./deploy.sh | tee deploy.log       # see it and save it

Pitfalls
  - Order matters: > file 2>&1 puts both in the file, 2>&1 > file leaves errors
    on the terminal.
  - > empties the file before the command starts: sort data > data leaves an
    empty file. Write elsewhere, then move it.
  - A pipe only carries stdout: errors still reach the terminal, unless you
    write 2>&1 | (or |& in bash).
  - set -o noclobber makes > refuse to overwrite an existing file; >| forces it.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 13.3 --why

